WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.445 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:02.445 --> 00:00:03.912 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.912 --> 00:00:05.868 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:05.868 --> 00:00:16.940 align:middle line:90%


00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:19.520 align:middle line:84%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: All
right, we'll get started.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:22.560 align:middle line:84%
See, last time we introduced
you to the definition

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.040 align:middle line:90%
of martingales.

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:27.680 align:middle line:84%
And what we're going to
do the first part today

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:32.960 align:middle line:84%
is to see how useful
and powerful martingale

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:37.520 align:middle line:84%
properties are and solving
interesting questions

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:38.540 align:middle line:90%
and challenges.

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:43.840 align:middle line:84%
So just as a reminder, if
we have a stochastic process

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:48.120 align:middle line:84%
xn, which is just a sequence
of random variables,

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:52.680 align:middle line:84%
generally the index n
corresponds to time.

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:58.040 align:middle line:84%
Then we can consider a
derived process capital

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:03.420 align:middle line:84%
M sub n, which is a function
of the first n values

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:06.100 align:middle line:90%
of the stochastic process x.

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:12.180 align:middle line:84%
And we say that this second
process, this derived process,

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:18.500 align:middle line:84%
is a martingale if the expected
value into the future from time

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:24.660 align:middle line:84%
n minus 1 is simply equal
to the time n minus 1 value.

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:32.020 align:middle line:84%
So with the process
evolving over time,

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:35.480 align:middle line:90%
we have an M sub n process.

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:40.100 align:middle line:84%
Basically, at time n
minus 1 we have a value,

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:44.420 align:middle line:84%
our prediction is that
the expected value of that

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:49.100 align:middle line:90%
in the future is Mn minus 1.

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:51.700 align:middle line:90%
And so some examples of these.

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:55.940 align:middle line:84%
We went through last time
a simple random walk.

00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:59.590 align:middle line:84%
And with the simple
random walk, we

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:05.190 align:middle line:84%
end up having realizations
of this martingale

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:10.550 align:middle line:84%
that evolve from, say,
a starting point 0.

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:14.870 align:middle line:84%
And what's critical
with these realizations

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:21.470 align:middle line:84%
is that the future paths
that have yet to be graphed

00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:25.950 align:middle line:84%
will have average value
equal to the last point

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:28.030 align:middle line:90%
on these realizations.

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:31.790 align:middle line:84%
So there's a notion of
there being flatness

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:37.502 align:middle line:90%
in the martingale expectations.

00:02:37.502 --> 00:02:43.190 align:middle line:84%
With the example here
of a simple random walk

00:02:43.190 --> 00:02:47.470 align:middle line:84%
with equal probability of
going up or down one step,

00:02:47.470 --> 00:02:52.350 align:middle line:84%
one can graph the
standard deviations up

00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:56.490 align:middle line:84%
from the average 0
for the processes.

00:02:56.490 --> 00:03:03.250 align:middle line:84%
And basically the realizations
of the random walk

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:06.330 align:middle line:84%
will, by the central
limit theorem,

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:09.570 align:middle line:84%
converge to a
normal distribution

00:03:09.570 --> 00:03:12.970 align:middle line:84%
at any given time point
far into the future.

00:03:12.970 --> 00:03:19.250 align:middle line:84%
Now, this second
example of a martingale

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:22.770 align:middle line:84%
where we have a random
walk Sn but then

00:03:22.770 --> 00:03:27.970 align:middle line:84%
we square it and subtract
n times the sigma squared,

00:03:27.970 --> 00:03:30.290 align:middle line:84%
the variance of each
of the steps, this

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:32.330 align:middle line:90%
is also a martingale.

00:03:32.330 --> 00:03:37.885 align:middle line:84%
And so looking at different
realizations of this martingale,

00:03:37.885 --> 00:03:40.010 align:middle line:84%
we can see that it's very,
very different from that

00:03:40.010 --> 00:03:42.970 align:middle line:90%
of a simple random walk.

00:03:42.970 --> 00:03:48.290 align:middle line:84%
And importantly,
there's basically

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:52.650 align:middle line:84%
of a lower limit
of the process, it

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:58.860 align:middle line:84%
appears, for fixed n and
an unbounded upper limit.

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:04.700 align:middle line:84%
But this process as
well is a martingale.

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:06.420 align:middle line:90%
And let's see.

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:09.260 align:middle line:84%
A different martingale
can be defined,

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:15.420 align:middle line:84%
not in terms of random
walks with steps that add,

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:23.240 align:middle line:84%
but with random shifts
with factors x1 to xn.

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:28.300 align:middle line:84%
So if we have Mn being
the product of x1 to xn,

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:33.980 align:middle line:84%
where the x's are independent
random variables with mean value

00:04:33.980 --> 00:04:40.380 align:middle line:84%
1, then this particular
process M sub n

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:44.140 align:middle line:84%
will be a martingale with
respect to the original series.

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:52.540 align:middle line:84%
So here's an example of
our Bernoulli factors,

00:04:52.540 --> 00:05:00.440 align:middle line:84%
which are with 50%
probability 1.5 and with 50%

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:03.320 align:middle line:90%
probability 0.5.

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:09.400 align:middle line:84%
This would correspond
to the change in wealth

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:13.440 align:middle line:84%
from betting your wealth
in a lottery where

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:20.080 align:middle line:84%
you either make 50% or lose
50% with equal probability.

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:27.220 align:middle line:84%
And here is the graph of a
realization over 50 steps.

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:30.400 align:middle line:90%
Here's other realizations.

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:36.200 align:middle line:84%
And what's interesting
to see is that

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:40.240 align:middle line:84%
with this particular
martingale, there

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:45.120 align:middle line:84%
are opportunities to, basically,
if you're playing this lottery

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:51.260 align:middle line:84%
game at each step, to be able
to grow your wealth to 5 or 10

00:05:51.260 --> 00:05:54.860 align:middle line:90%
times its initial value.

00:05:54.860 --> 00:06:00.780 align:middle line:84%
But with moderate or
even high probability,

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:04.780 align:middle line:84%
you actually have lost
almost all your wealth.

00:06:04.780 --> 00:06:08.460 align:middle line:84%
So yet another
example, and this one

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:13.460 align:middle line:84%
is really rather
curious to introduce,

00:06:13.460 --> 00:06:18.340 align:middle line:84%
but it shows you how we can
be flexible in generating

00:06:18.340 --> 00:06:21.260 align:middle line:90%
or constructing martingales.

00:06:21.260 --> 00:06:28.100 align:middle line:84%
If we have the yn
sequence being iid

00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:36.260 align:middle line:84%
random variables and the
moment generating function

00:06:36.260 --> 00:06:47.900 align:middle line:84%
e to the lambda yn
or e to the lambda y,

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:52.910 align:middle line:84%
yn is equal to the moment
generating function.

00:06:52.910 --> 00:06:56.750 align:middle line:90%
Or that's e to the ty.

00:06:56.750 --> 00:07:00.670 align:middle line:84%
In our notation, we're going
to use a phi of lambda here

00:07:00.670 --> 00:07:01.810 align:middle line:90%
or phi of t.

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:04.470 align:middle line:90%


00:07:04.470 --> 00:07:15.270 align:middle line:84%
Then if we consider
taking xn equaling e

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:22.830 align:middle line:84%
to the lambda yn divided
by phi of lambda,

00:07:22.830 --> 00:07:30.310 align:middle line:84%
then this xn will
be a martingale.

00:07:30.310 --> 00:07:34.230 align:middle line:84%
Or this xn, rather,
will be factors that

00:07:34.230 --> 00:07:39.030 align:middle line:90%
have expectation equaling 1.

00:07:39.030 --> 00:07:45.470 align:middle line:84%
And if we take the product
of these x's, we basically

00:07:45.470 --> 00:07:53.170 align:middle line:84%
have M sub n equaling e to the
lambda times the sum of the yn's

00:07:53.170 --> 00:08:03.210 align:middle line:84%
divided by phi of lambda
to the n-th power.

00:08:03.210 --> 00:08:06.290 align:middle line:90%
This will also be a martingale.

00:08:06.290 --> 00:08:11.010 align:middle line:84%
And what we'll see towards
the middle of this lecture

00:08:11.010 --> 00:08:13.970 align:middle line:84%
is that this
particular martingale

00:08:13.970 --> 00:08:16.610 align:middle line:84%
can be used to
solve probabilities

00:08:16.610 --> 00:08:20.490 align:middle line:84%
of gambler's ruin, when
you have gambles that

00:08:20.490 --> 00:08:27.350 align:middle line:84%
have different probabilities
of success versus failure,

00:08:27.350 --> 00:08:31.850 align:middle line:90%
not necessarily equal fair bets.

00:08:31.850 --> 00:08:32.470 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:08:32.470 --> 00:08:36.370 align:middle line:84%
Well, with martingales,
it turns out

00:08:36.370 --> 00:08:41.770 align:middle line:84%
to be very useful
to consider what

00:08:41.770 --> 00:08:51.340 align:middle line:84%
we call the information sets on
the subsequence of variables x1

00:08:51.340 --> 00:08:52.980 align:middle line:90%
through xn.

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:59.660 align:middle line:84%
So if we think of information
progressing over time,

00:08:59.660 --> 00:09:03.860 align:middle line:84%
the information up
to time little n

00:09:03.860 --> 00:09:07.980 align:middle line:84%
can be given by the
outcomes of the x process.

00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:15.200 align:middle line:84%
And we can consider
expectations of random variables

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:17.740 align:middle line:90%
given this information set.

00:09:17.740 --> 00:09:21.360 align:middle line:84%
And the kinds of
random variables

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:26.200 align:middle line:84%
we might be interested in,
z, can correspond to, say,

00:09:26.200 --> 00:09:31.940 align:middle line:84%
trading strategies based on
the outcomes of asset prices

00:09:31.940 --> 00:09:34.700 align:middle line:90%
progressing over time.

00:09:34.700 --> 00:09:44.420 align:middle line:84%
And if we have, let's see, if
this original information set is

00:09:44.420 --> 00:09:50.320 align:middle line:84%
represented in terms
of a martingale,

00:09:50.320 --> 00:10:04.040 align:middle line:84%
then we can consider functions
of the first n values given

00:10:04.040 --> 00:10:14.380 align:middle line:84%
by y, and relating to this
function of the first n values,

00:10:14.380 --> 00:10:19.520 align:middle line:84%
which could be, say, the trading
gains from applying a trading

00:10:19.520 --> 00:10:24.360 align:middle line:84%
strategy with asset
prices given by the x's.

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:29.640 align:middle line:84%
It will be very useful to
use random variables that

00:10:29.640 --> 00:10:32.840 align:middle line:90%
are called non-anticipating.

00:10:32.840 --> 00:10:37.440 align:middle line:84%
And so a non-anticipating
random variable

00:10:37.440 --> 00:10:43.640 align:middle line:84%
is a random variable
An that is defined

00:10:43.640 --> 00:10:47.040 align:middle line:90%
signed by the information set.

00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:51.700 align:middle line:84%
It's non-random given the
information set of the first n

00:10:51.700 --> 00:10:54.020 align:middle line:90%
minus 1 x's.

00:10:54.020 --> 00:11:02.020 align:middle line:84%
So if we wanted to represent
the outcome of making trading

00:11:02.020 --> 00:11:07.500 align:middle line:84%
decisions using the
first n minus 1 values,

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:11.500 align:middle line:84%
this An could characterize
how we might invest

00:11:11.500 --> 00:11:15.220 align:middle line:90%
for the n-th trading period.

00:11:15.220 --> 00:11:21.420 align:middle line:84%
Now, with such a non
anticipating random variable,

00:11:21.420 --> 00:11:30.420 align:middle line:84%
we can consider a transformation
of the original martingale,

00:11:30.420 --> 00:11:38.260 align:middle line:84%
given by M's with no tildes,
and consider a transformation

00:11:38.260 --> 00:11:42.910 align:middle line:84%
of the Mn martingale
by considering

00:11:42.910 --> 00:11:46.610 align:middle line:90%
this decomposition of M tilde.

00:11:46.610 --> 00:11:50.030 align:middle line:90%
So we have an initial value M0.

00:11:50.030 --> 00:11:56.630 align:middle line:84%
Then we have the non
anticipating variable A1 that

00:11:56.630 --> 00:12:00.390 align:middle line:84%
depends only on
information at time 0 times

00:12:00.390 --> 00:12:05.390 align:middle line:84%
the increment of the Martingale
from time 0 to time 1.

00:12:05.390 --> 00:12:07.570 align:middle line:90%
And we add successive terms.

00:12:07.570 --> 00:12:11.790 align:middle line:84%
So these A's basically
are random variables

00:12:11.790 --> 00:12:14.910 align:middle line:90%
at the beginning of time.

00:12:14.910 --> 00:12:27.270 align:middle line:84%
But after observing, say, j
time periods, the Aj, let's see,

00:12:27.270 --> 00:12:28.430 align:middle line:90%
will be--

00:12:28.430 --> 00:12:32.110 align:middle line:84%
or j minus 1 time
periods, the Aj

00:12:32.110 --> 00:12:38.000 align:middle line:84%
will be defined and non-random
going from time j to j plus 1.

00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:41.650 align:middle line:90%


00:12:41.650 --> 00:12:48.970 align:middle line:84%
And if you were to look at
this M tilde sub n definition,

00:12:48.970 --> 00:12:54.970 align:middle line:84%
we basically have terms
that are given by increments

00:12:54.970 --> 00:13:00.850 align:middle line:84%
of the martingale process,
where each of those increments

00:13:00.850 --> 00:13:04.850 align:middle line:90%
has expectation equal to 0.

00:13:04.850 --> 00:13:10.850 align:middle line:84%
And so if we take the
expectation of those

00:13:10.850 --> 00:13:19.290 align:middle line:84%
from time 0, then by conditional
expectation properties,

00:13:19.290 --> 00:13:27.970 align:middle line:84%
the A's are fixed given the
information up to time or j

00:13:27.970 --> 00:13:35.370 align:middle line:84%
minus 1, and then the variation
of the martingale process

00:13:35.370 --> 00:13:41.060 align:middle line:84%
from time j minus 1 to
j has expectation 0.

00:13:41.060 --> 00:13:45.780 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, the martingale
transform theorem

00:13:45.780 --> 00:13:51.400 align:middle line:84%
says that this M tilde n
process is a martingale

00:13:51.400 --> 00:13:57.260 align:middle line:84%
also with respect to
the information sets fn.

00:13:57.260 --> 00:14:00.980 align:middle line:84%
And we often call this
collection of information

00:14:00.980 --> 00:14:04.060 align:middle line:90%
sets a filtration.

00:14:04.060 --> 00:14:07.800 align:middle line:84%
And so when we think
of different processes,

00:14:07.800 --> 00:14:14.100 align:middle line:84%
different realizations of
a process up to time n,

00:14:14.100 --> 00:14:19.060 align:middle line:84%
then whatever the
history is prior

00:14:19.060 --> 00:14:25.380 align:middle line:84%
to n, that is the information
set we have available.

00:14:25.380 --> 00:14:32.660 align:middle line:84%
And as we observe successive
values of the original Mn

00:14:32.660 --> 00:14:39.080 align:middle line:84%
process, we're basically
adding more information

00:14:39.080 --> 00:14:41.920 align:middle line:90%
about the realizations.

00:14:41.920 --> 00:14:45.920 align:middle line:90%
And that's how it progresses.

00:14:45.920 --> 00:14:51.560 align:middle line:84%
Now, with martingales, an
important concept for us

00:14:51.560 --> 00:14:57.840 align:middle line:84%
is a stopping time random
variable, which we'll call tau.

00:14:57.840 --> 00:15:02.000 align:middle line:84%
And so what the
stopping time variable

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:11.120 align:middle line:84%
is, is a random variable
that characterizes

00:15:11.120 --> 00:15:16.840 align:middle line:84%
whether, let's see, or
I guess at any time n,

00:15:16.840 --> 00:15:22.840 align:middle line:84%
we know whether the stopping
time has occurred or not.

00:15:22.840 --> 00:15:30.360 align:middle line:84%
So the event of the stopping
time being less than or equal

00:15:30.360 --> 00:15:37.260 align:middle line:84%
to n steps, that has to be
known in the information set up

00:15:37.260 --> 00:15:38.200 align:middle line:90%
to time n.

00:15:38.200 --> 00:15:42.940 align:middle line:84%
So the notation here
is a little bit tricky.

00:15:42.940 --> 00:15:51.980 align:middle line:84%
We think of this set or event
being the underlying probability

00:15:51.980 --> 00:15:58.540 align:middle line:84%
distribution states where
the tau variable is less than

00:15:58.540 --> 00:16:00.620 align:middle line:90%
or equal to n.

00:16:00.620 --> 00:16:08.620 align:middle line:84%
And at any time n, we know
whether this set has occurred

00:16:08.620 --> 00:16:10.180 align:middle line:90%
or not.

00:16:10.180 --> 00:16:16.340 align:middle line:84%
So the stopping time
is a random variable,

00:16:16.340 --> 00:16:19.120 align:middle line:90%
and it could be infinite.

00:16:19.120 --> 00:16:24.100 align:middle line:84%
So it takes values on
the set S varying from 0

00:16:24.100 --> 00:16:29.980 align:middle line:84%
up to possibly infinity if
the process never stops.

00:16:29.980 --> 00:16:34.630 align:middle line:84%
Now, a way of trying
to help explain

00:16:34.630 --> 00:16:40.010 align:middle line:84%
this is with a stopping
time random variable,

00:16:40.010 --> 00:16:46.610 align:middle line:84%
we have different omegas
in the probability space,

00:16:46.610 --> 00:16:52.150 align:middle line:84%
and the omegas can be thought of
as indexing all possible paths.

00:16:52.150 --> 00:16:56.750 align:middle line:84%
So with different realizations,
we have a different omega.

00:16:56.750 --> 00:17:07.230 align:middle line:84%
And so with these paths, our
expectation or our event E,

00:17:07.230 --> 00:17:12.550 align:middle line:84%
which is the set of paths
for which the stopping

00:17:12.550 --> 00:17:17.510 align:middle line:84%
time evaluated on that path
is less than or equal to n,

00:17:17.510 --> 00:17:24.470 align:middle line:84%
this event is known to either
have occurred or not to occur.

00:17:24.470 --> 00:17:30.910 align:middle line:84%
So we have the indicator
of the path being

00:17:30.910 --> 00:17:35.570 align:middle line:90%
in this set E is either 0 or 1.

00:17:35.570 --> 00:17:43.930 align:middle line:84%
And so we can work with
this random variable,

00:17:43.930 --> 00:17:50.130 align:middle line:84%
the indicator of whether the
stopping time has occurred

00:17:50.130 --> 00:17:51.410 align:middle line:90%
or not.

00:17:51.410 --> 00:17:58.490 align:middle line:84%
And with this
setup, we can think

00:17:58.490 --> 00:18:05.650 align:middle line:84%
of the process that is governed
by a stopping time tau.

00:18:05.650 --> 00:18:10.250 align:middle line:84%
We can represent the
process at the stopped time

00:18:10.250 --> 00:18:16.010 align:middle line:84%
tau as the sum of
the realizations xn

00:18:16.010 --> 00:18:21.650 align:middle line:84%
times a simple indicator of
whether the stopping time equals

00:18:21.650 --> 00:18:23.850 align:middle line:90%
n or not.

00:18:23.850 --> 00:18:28.210 align:middle line:84%
So we basically have
our stopped process

00:18:28.210 --> 00:18:37.100 align:middle line:84%
being a sum of weights times the
xn's, where those weights are

00:18:37.100 --> 00:18:38.560 align:middle line:90%
actually 0 or 1.

00:18:38.560 --> 00:18:41.100 align:middle line:90%


00:18:41.100 --> 00:18:45.940 align:middle line:84%
Now, in the mathematics of
dealing with stopping times,

00:18:45.940 --> 00:18:53.780 align:middle line:84%
the potential for infinite
values of the stopping time

00:18:53.780 --> 00:18:56.020 align:middle line:90%
makes things complicated.

00:18:56.020 --> 00:19:01.380 align:middle line:84%
And what we can do is
consider the minimum

00:19:01.380 --> 00:19:06.740 align:middle line:84%
of n and the stopping
time tau denoting that

00:19:06.740 --> 00:19:12.220 align:middle line:84%
by n caret tau, so the
minimum of n and tau.

00:19:12.220 --> 00:19:18.140 align:middle line:84%
And so if we have
a finite value n,

00:19:18.140 --> 00:19:22.380 align:middle line:84%
this truncated
stopping time is going

00:19:22.380 --> 00:19:28.580 align:middle line:84%
to be a measurable random
variable with respect

00:19:28.580 --> 00:19:36.320 align:middle line:84%
to the information set script
FM, information set FM for M

00:19:36.320 --> 00:19:38.040 align:middle line:90%
greater than or equal to n.

00:19:38.040 --> 00:19:48.400 align:middle line:84%
So if we imagine basically
this time increasing,

00:19:48.400 --> 00:19:54.840 align:middle line:84%
and we think of the
minimum of n and tau,

00:19:54.840 --> 00:20:00.760 align:middle line:84%
then we will be
considering processes

00:20:00.760 --> 00:20:08.200 align:middle line:84%
that maybe stop for a given
tau, for a given realization.

00:20:08.200 --> 00:20:11.680 align:middle line:84%
Or if they don't really
stop until later,

00:20:11.680 --> 00:20:15.920 align:middle line:84%
we just consider the
value at the n point.

00:20:15.920 --> 00:20:22.880 align:middle line:84%
Now, this theorem can be
proven with this argument.

00:20:22.880 --> 00:20:29.860 align:middle line:84%
And what you can see
is that we define

00:20:29.860 --> 00:20:38.740 align:middle line:84%
our non-anticipating variables
to be the indicator of tau,

00:20:38.740 --> 00:20:41.340 align:middle line:84%
the stopping time being
greater than or equal to k.

00:20:41.340 --> 00:20:47.540 align:middle line:84%
And so substituting
that in to the formula

00:20:47.540 --> 00:20:58.100 align:middle line:84%
for the decomposition, we end
up getting that this stopped

00:20:58.100 --> 00:21:02.540 align:middle line:84%
martingale at time,
the minimum of n or tau

00:21:02.540 --> 00:21:06.860 align:middle line:84%
is actually equal to the sum of
these non-anticipating variables

00:21:06.860 --> 00:21:10.300 align:middle line:84%
times the increments
of the martingale.

00:21:10.300 --> 00:21:17.260 align:middle line:84%
So this is easily a
martingale transform

00:21:17.260 --> 00:21:20.900 align:middle line:84%
and satisfies that
martingale transform theorem.

00:21:20.900 --> 00:21:27.630 align:middle line:84%
So this stopped martingale
at the minimum of n and tau

00:21:27.630 --> 00:21:29.670 align:middle line:90%
is also a martingale.

00:21:29.670 --> 00:21:35.970 align:middle line:84%
What we then want to
do is consider taking--

00:21:35.970 --> 00:21:36.470 align:middle line:90%
let's see.

00:21:36.470 --> 00:21:39.490 align:middle line:84%
I'm not sure if we do
it here shortly or not,

00:21:39.490 --> 00:21:44.750 align:middle line:84%
but we'll basically
let little n grow large

00:21:44.750 --> 00:21:48.250 align:middle line:84%
and argue that in
the limiting case,

00:21:48.250 --> 00:21:53.150 align:middle line:84%
we basically have the stopped
martingale without the minimum

00:21:53.150 --> 00:21:55.030 align:middle line:90%
of n in tau.

00:21:55.030 --> 00:21:58.830 align:middle line:84%
Well, let's look at
the simple random

00:21:58.830 --> 00:22:05.430 align:middle line:84%
walk we had before where the
steps are plus 1 or negative 1.

00:22:05.430 --> 00:22:11.830 align:middle line:84%
And we consider Sn being
the sum of the steps.

00:22:11.830 --> 00:22:18.630 align:middle line:84%
This can be used to characterize
the problem of playing

00:22:18.630 --> 00:22:24.030 align:middle line:84%
against an opponent and
betting $1 with each iteration

00:22:24.030 --> 00:22:25.450 align:middle line:90%
of the game.

00:22:25.450 --> 00:22:29.570 align:middle line:84%
And you either win
or lose that dollar

00:22:29.570 --> 00:22:32.630 align:middle line:90%
each iteration of the game.

00:22:32.630 --> 00:22:46.610 align:middle line:84%
And we can consider having
a hit level for the sum Sn

00:22:46.610 --> 00:22:49.850 align:middle line:84%
being equal to
plus A or minus B.

00:22:49.850 --> 00:22:56.890 align:middle line:84%
And so basically, this
reflects whether we

00:22:56.890 --> 00:23:06.450 align:middle line:84%
beat our opponent or not,
where our opponent has a,

00:23:06.450 --> 00:23:12.410 align:middle line:84%
well, let's see, if
we have a bankroll B

00:23:12.410 --> 00:23:16.210 align:middle line:84%
and our opponent
has a bankroll A,

00:23:16.210 --> 00:23:22.420 align:middle line:84%
do we win the entire bankroll
of our opponent and hit A first?

00:23:22.420 --> 00:23:25.340 align:middle line:90%


00:23:25.340 --> 00:23:28.980 align:middle line:84%
So we have this
problem, and we want

00:23:28.980 --> 00:23:34.950 align:middle line:84%
to know what's the likelihood
of basically player one losing.

00:23:34.950 --> 00:23:37.460 align:middle line:90%


00:23:37.460 --> 00:23:40.980 align:middle line:90%
And the SN is a martingale.

00:23:40.980 --> 00:23:52.500 align:middle line:84%
And tau, the time at which the
sum Sn equals plus A or minus B

00:23:52.500 --> 00:23:54.500 align:middle line:90%
is a stopping time.

00:23:54.500 --> 00:23:59.140 align:middle line:84%
And so this is a martingale
by the stopping time theorem.

00:23:59.140 --> 00:24:02.500 align:middle line:84%
And with such a
martingale we have

00:24:02.500 --> 00:24:10.700 align:middle line:84%
that the expected value
of S at the stopped time

00:24:10.700 --> 00:24:16.140 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the expected
value initially.

00:24:16.140 --> 00:24:20.620 align:middle line:90%
And that is equal to 0.

00:24:20.620 --> 00:24:26.640 align:middle line:84%
Starting out at time
0, Sn is equal to 0.

00:24:26.640 --> 00:24:32.160 align:middle line:84%
And so if we take the limit
as n goes to infinity,

00:24:32.160 --> 00:24:37.160 align:middle line:84%
this converges to
the random variable S

00:24:37.160 --> 00:24:40.000 align:middle line:90%
tau with probability 1.

00:24:40.000 --> 00:24:47.680 align:middle line:84%
And this is proven partly
by showing that the stopping

00:24:47.680 --> 00:24:49.640 align:middle line:90%
time can't be infinite.

00:24:49.640 --> 00:24:54.920 align:middle line:84%
But if we assume that
these results are valid,

00:24:54.920 --> 00:25:00.560 align:middle line:84%
then we have 0 is equal to
the expected value of S tau.

00:25:00.560 --> 00:25:06.720 align:middle line:84%
And if we write out what S tau
is, at the stopping time tau,

00:25:06.720 --> 00:25:11.400 align:middle line:84%
it's either going to
equal A times the S tau

00:25:11.400 --> 00:25:20.780 align:middle line:84%
hitting A or minus B times the
stopped value equaling minus B.

00:25:20.780 --> 00:25:25.740 align:middle line:84%
And if we take expectations
of this equation,

00:25:25.740 --> 00:25:31.820 align:middle line:84%
we basically have 0 is equal
to A times the probability

00:25:31.820 --> 00:25:37.140 align:middle line:84%
that we hit the level plus A
minus B times the probability

00:25:37.140 --> 00:25:41.580 align:middle line:84%
that we hit minus B. And those
probabilities are complements

00:25:41.580 --> 00:25:43.940 align:middle line:90%
of each other.

00:25:43.940 --> 00:25:48.940 align:middle line:84%
So the chance that we hit
minus B at the stop time

00:25:48.940 --> 00:25:52.060 align:middle line:84%
is 1 minus the
probability that we hit A.

00:25:52.060 --> 00:25:56.500 align:middle line:84%
So using these
relationships, we get

00:25:56.500 --> 00:26:05.740 align:middle line:84%
that the probability that we hit
A first is B over A plus B. Yes?

00:26:05.740 --> 00:26:08.380 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So on the previous
slide, can I just check,

00:26:08.380 --> 00:26:10.200 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if it
might be an error

00:26:10.200 --> 00:26:12.540 align:middle line:84%
or if I don't
understand something.

00:26:12.540 --> 00:26:16.370 align:middle line:84%
Is it meant to say
solve for S tau plus A

00:26:16.370 --> 00:26:17.620 align:middle line:90%
where it says problem in bold?

00:26:17.620 --> 00:26:20.830 align:middle line:90%


00:26:20.830 --> 00:26:22.190 align:middle line:90%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: Let's see.

00:26:22.190 --> 00:26:23.490 align:middle line:90%
Can you ask the question again?

00:26:23.490 --> 00:26:25.750 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So where it says
problem in bold [INAUDIBLE]

00:26:25.750 --> 00:26:29.150 align:middle line:84%
and then problem solved, is it
meant to say probability that S

00:26:29.150 --> 00:26:29.887 align:middle line:90%
tau--

00:26:29.887 --> 00:26:30.970 align:middle line:90%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: Oh, yes.

00:26:30.970 --> 00:26:37.590 align:middle line:84%
It's probably the S tau equals
plus A. Yes, definitely.

00:26:37.590 --> 00:26:39.190 align:middle line:90%
No, no, that's fine.

00:26:39.190 --> 00:26:39.690 align:middle line:90%
That's fine.

00:26:39.690 --> 00:26:41.470 align:middle line:90%
There's always typos.

00:26:41.470 --> 00:26:46.070 align:middle line:84%
Fewer typos on slides than on
the Blackboard, I would say.

00:26:46.070 --> 00:26:50.310 align:middle line:84%
That's the benefit of having
the slides, and easier to fix.

00:26:50.310 --> 00:26:50.890 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:26:50.890 --> 00:26:52.230 align:middle line:90%
Thank you for that.

00:26:52.230 --> 00:26:52.810 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:26:52.810 --> 00:26:56.790 align:middle line:84%
So we get this really nice
formula for the likelihood

00:26:56.790 --> 00:27:05.830 align:middle line:84%
of hitting A before hitting
minus B. And so by symmetry,

00:27:05.830 --> 00:27:10.270 align:middle line:84%
if A equals B, then
this is simply 1/2.

00:27:10.270 --> 00:27:18.570 align:middle line:84%
But if the player B
has a larger bankroll

00:27:18.570 --> 00:27:25.248 align:middle line:84%
than the chance of taking
all the money from A,

00:27:25.248 --> 00:27:30.050 align:middle line:90%
basically goes to 1.

00:27:30.050 --> 00:27:35.490 align:middle line:84%
So it makes sense that if you're
going to gamble with fair odds,

00:27:35.490 --> 00:27:37.530 align:middle line:84%
you have an advantage
if you have a bigger

00:27:37.530 --> 00:27:40.450 align:middle line:90%
bankroll than your opponent.

00:27:40.450 --> 00:27:46.210 align:middle line:84%
Now, this second
example of martingales,

00:27:46.210 --> 00:27:54.010 align:middle line:84%
which is the square of the
random walk Sn minus n, this

00:27:54.010 --> 00:27:57.210 align:middle line:90%
is a martingale as well.

00:27:57.210 --> 00:28:03.170 align:middle line:84%
And if we look at
this Martingale,

00:28:03.170 --> 00:28:09.790 align:middle line:84%
it's bounded by the max of A
squared B squared plus tau.

00:28:09.790 --> 00:28:14.050 align:middle line:84%
So we have a bounded
transformation

00:28:14.050 --> 00:28:16.980 align:middle line:90%
of the original Sn.

00:28:16.980 --> 00:28:25.460 align:middle line:84%
And so this martingale has
expectation 0 for all n.

00:28:25.460 --> 00:28:30.380 align:middle line:84%
And so if we look
at the expectation

00:28:30.380 --> 00:28:40.060 align:middle line:84%
of M tau, this transformation
of the original martingale Sn,

00:28:40.060 --> 00:28:47.540 align:middle line:84%
it has 0 expectation, which is
equal to the expected value of S

00:28:47.540 --> 00:28:51.500 align:middle line:84%
tau squared minus the
expected value of tau.

00:28:51.500 --> 00:29:01.140 align:middle line:84%
And if we look at what is the
expected value of S tau squared,

00:29:01.140 --> 00:29:06.620 align:middle line:84%
so this term here,
we have that that's

00:29:06.620 --> 00:29:13.340 align:middle line:84%
equal to being stopped
at plus A times A squared

00:29:13.340 --> 00:29:15.360 align:middle line:84%
plus the probability
of being stopped

00:29:15.360 --> 00:29:18.240 align:middle line:90%
at minus B times B squared.

00:29:18.240 --> 00:29:22.340 align:middle line:84%
And this expectation
is equal to 0.

00:29:22.340 --> 00:29:27.640 align:middle line:84%
So we can actually solve for
the expected value of tau.

00:29:27.640 --> 00:29:31.920 align:middle line:84%
It's simply equal to the
expected value of S tau squared.

00:29:31.920 --> 00:29:38.240 align:middle line:84%
So plugging everything in,
it gets a little tricky

00:29:38.240 --> 00:29:39.780 align:middle line:90%
until we simplify.

00:29:39.780 --> 00:29:43.200 align:middle line:84%
And it's simply the
product of A times B.

00:29:43.200 --> 00:29:51.280 align:middle line:84%
So the amount of time it
takes to defeat the opponent

00:29:51.280 --> 00:29:55.400 align:middle line:84%
has an expected time
equal to A times B.

00:29:55.400 --> 00:30:01.740 align:middle line:84%
So if you're gambling
in such a situation,

00:30:01.740 --> 00:30:05.720 align:middle line:84%
the size of your bankroll times
the size of the other person's

00:30:05.720 --> 00:30:08.640 align:middle line:84%
bankroll estimates
the approximate time

00:30:08.640 --> 00:30:16.460 align:middle line:84%
it will take to finish the game,
with one dominating the other.

00:30:16.460 --> 00:30:21.460 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, what's characteristic
of these examples is

00:30:21.460 --> 00:30:26.620 align:middle line:84%
we have a martingale transform
of the original series.

00:30:26.620 --> 00:30:32.900 align:middle line:84%
It has a 0 expectation
as it's defined.

00:30:32.900 --> 00:30:37.980 align:middle line:84%
And that 0 expectation applies
for all time points as well as

00:30:37.980 --> 00:30:41.060 align:middle line:90%
the stopping time.

00:30:41.060 --> 00:30:43.100 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:30:43.100 --> 00:30:48.060 align:middle line:84%
Well, a really
challenging problem

00:30:48.060 --> 00:30:54.060 align:middle line:84%
is to consider the
random walk with bias.

00:30:54.060 --> 00:31:01.220 align:middle line:84%
So we consider steps plus 1
or minus 1 with probability p

00:31:01.220 --> 00:31:04.260 align:middle line:90%
and 1 minus p respectively.

00:31:04.260 --> 00:31:11.670 align:middle line:84%
And then we're
interested in what

00:31:11.670 --> 00:31:16.770 align:middle line:84%
is the probability of hitting
plus A, say, in this case.

00:31:16.770 --> 00:31:21.950 align:middle line:84%
Certainly, as p
increases, the probability

00:31:21.950 --> 00:31:24.936 align:middle line:84%
of hitting plus A
first increases.

00:31:24.936 --> 00:31:28.070 align:middle line:90%


00:31:28.070 --> 00:31:29.410 align:middle line:90%
But how does it do so?

00:31:29.410 --> 00:31:35.043 align:middle line:84%
Now, some of you have taken
stochastic processes already.

00:31:35.043 --> 00:31:36.710 align:middle line:84%
Or how many people
have taken the course

00:31:36.710 --> 00:31:38.330 align:middle line:90%
in stochastic processes?

00:31:38.330 --> 00:31:41.470 align:middle line:90%
Maybe just a couple.

00:31:41.470 --> 00:31:47.870 align:middle line:84%
Do you recall this problem
being covered in your course?

00:31:47.870 --> 00:31:53.590 align:middle line:84%
And when I taught the
stochastic processes course

00:31:53.590 --> 00:31:59.190 align:middle line:84%
about five or six years
ago, the way this was solved

00:31:59.190 --> 00:32:07.030 align:middle line:84%
was by brute force understanding
the dynamics of the process

00:32:07.030 --> 00:32:14.130 align:middle line:84%
and doing computations
with first step analyses.

00:32:14.130 --> 00:32:19.290 align:middle line:84%
And the solution was
really quite complex,

00:32:19.290 --> 00:32:21.690 align:middle line:90%
but it got the right answer.

00:32:21.690 --> 00:32:23.690 align:middle line:84%
What's really neat
about martingales

00:32:23.690 --> 00:32:28.810 align:middle line:84%
is that we can actually solve
this problem pretty elegantly.

00:32:28.810 --> 00:32:32.330 align:middle line:84%
And so we're going to use this
moment generating function

00:32:32.330 --> 00:32:34.450 align:middle line:90%
transformation.

00:32:34.450 --> 00:32:39.110 align:middle line:84%
So our xn's have a moment
generating function,

00:32:39.110 --> 00:32:44.410 align:middle line:84%
which is p, the probability of
plus 1, times e to the lambda

00:32:44.410 --> 00:32:50.330 align:middle line:84%
times plus 1 plus q, the
probability of negative 1 times

00:32:50.330 --> 00:32:53.630 align:middle line:84%
e to the lambda
times negative 1.

00:32:53.630 --> 00:32:57.010 align:middle line:84%
So this definition of the
moment generating function

00:32:57.010 --> 00:32:59.970 align:middle line:90%
is very straightforward.

00:32:59.970 --> 00:33:04.230 align:middle line:84%
If we take e to the
lambda xn divided by phi,

00:33:04.230 --> 00:33:07.130 align:middle line:84%
these are independent
random variables that

00:33:07.130 --> 00:33:11.940 align:middle line:90%
have expectation equal to 1.

00:33:11.940 --> 00:33:19.100 align:middle line:84%
And we can define n then to be
the product of the first n yi's.

00:33:19.100 --> 00:33:24.940 align:middle line:84%
And this will be a
martingale by our example 4.

00:33:24.940 --> 00:33:36.500 align:middle line:84%
And if we solve for the lambda
value, that makes phi of lambda

00:33:36.500 --> 00:33:39.420 align:middle line:90%
equal to 1.

00:33:39.420 --> 00:33:44.580 align:middle line:84%
Then for that fixed
value of lambda,

00:33:44.580 --> 00:33:48.960 align:middle line:84%
this Mn is going
to be a martingale.

00:33:48.960 --> 00:33:51.380 align:middle line:84%
It's actually a
martingale for any lambda.

00:33:51.380 --> 00:33:55.620 align:middle line:84%
But in addition, that special
case where phi of lambda

00:33:55.620 --> 00:33:57.620 align:middle line:90%
equals 1.

00:33:57.620 --> 00:34:02.140 align:middle line:84%
And so if we graph this
moment generating function

00:34:02.140 --> 00:34:10.520 align:middle line:84%
as a function of lambda, we
can see that it's equal to 1

00:34:10.520 --> 00:34:13.400 align:middle line:84%
when e to the lambda
is equal to q over p.

00:34:13.400 --> 00:34:17.000 align:middle line:90%


00:34:17.000 --> 00:34:22.920 align:middle line:84%
So Mn for this particular
lambda for which e to the lambda

00:34:22.920 --> 00:34:30.840 align:middle line:84%
equals q over p is simply
q over p to the power Sn.

00:34:30.840 --> 00:34:35.360 align:middle line:84%
And with that, we basically
have the initial value

00:34:35.360 --> 00:34:41.199 align:middle line:84%
of M at time 1, basically,
or at time 0 is 1.

00:34:41.199 --> 00:34:46.040 align:middle line:84%
And that should equal the
stopped value of q over p

00:34:46.040 --> 00:34:49.980 align:middle line:84%
to the power SN2 at
the stopped value.

00:34:49.980 --> 00:34:53.679 align:middle line:84%
So it's q over p to the
plus A times the probability

00:34:53.679 --> 00:34:56.840 align:middle line:84%
of stopping at A plus
q over p to the minus

00:34:56.840 --> 00:35:01.120 align:middle line:84%
B times the probability
of stopping at minus B.

00:35:01.120 --> 00:35:06.820 align:middle line:84%
And again, this equation
basically gives us

00:35:06.820 --> 00:35:10.740 align:middle line:84%
two terms that are
related to each other

00:35:10.740 --> 00:35:12.460 align:middle line:90%
that we can solve for.

00:35:12.460 --> 00:35:19.900 align:middle line:84%
So we get the probability that
this biased random walk stops

00:35:19.900 --> 00:35:24.620 align:middle line:84%
or hits plus A first is
simply the ratio of q

00:35:24.620 --> 00:35:30.900 align:middle line:84%
to p to the power of B minus 1
over the same ratio to the power

00:35:30.900 --> 00:35:41.660 align:middle line:84%
A plus B. So this result
is very direct and elegant.

00:35:41.660 --> 00:35:46.060 align:middle line:84%
If you look at stochastic
processes books that

00:35:46.060 --> 00:35:49.260 align:middle line:84%
try to solve this
biased random walk

00:35:49.260 --> 00:35:51.780 align:middle line:84%
problem without
martingale theory,

00:35:51.780 --> 00:35:54.290 align:middle line:84%
it's a bit more
involved and tricky.

00:35:54.290 --> 00:35:56.900 align:middle line:90%


00:35:56.900 --> 00:35:59.540 align:middle line:84%
All right, so here's
just some R code that

00:35:59.540 --> 00:36:04.430 align:middle line:84%
will graph these probabilities
as functions of A and B.

00:36:04.430 --> 00:36:08.270 align:middle line:84%
I think it's useful only just to
know that you can do something

00:36:08.270 --> 00:36:08.770 align:middle line:90%
like this.

00:36:08.770 --> 00:36:11.150 align:middle line:84%
You can graph the
probability surface

00:36:11.150 --> 00:36:14.990 align:middle line:84%
as it varies with the
hit levels A and B.

00:36:14.990 --> 00:36:21.230 align:middle line:84%
And here are contours of the
different probabilities, which

00:36:21.230 --> 00:36:23.390 align:middle line:90%
can also be graphed easily.

00:36:23.390 --> 00:36:30.190 align:middle line:84%
And that finishes this initial
discussion of martingales.

00:36:30.190 --> 00:36:38.190 align:middle line:84%
Now, a second topic in
stochastic processes

00:36:38.190 --> 00:36:45.670 align:middle line:84%
that is extremely important is
the concept of Markov processes.

00:36:45.670 --> 00:36:53.230 align:middle line:84%
And so if we think of a
general stochastic process xt,

00:36:53.230 --> 00:36:57.790 align:middle line:84%
then we will say that
the process is a Markov

00:36:57.790 --> 00:37:07.410 align:middle line:84%
process if for any
times of the process S

00:37:07.410 --> 00:37:17.410 align:middle line:84%
that's between u and t, that xt
will be independent of all xu's

00:37:17.410 --> 00:37:21.210 align:middle line:90%
where u is less than s.

00:37:21.210 --> 00:37:30.170 align:middle line:84%
So when we think of a time
scale, so we have u less than s

00:37:30.170 --> 00:37:31.190 align:middle line:90%
less than t.

00:37:31.190 --> 00:37:36.650 align:middle line:90%


00:37:36.650 --> 00:37:42.490 align:middle line:84%
If we imagine realizations
of a stochastic process,

00:37:42.490 --> 00:37:54.090 align:middle line:84%
and we are interested in
looking at the value of xt,

00:37:54.090 --> 00:38:01.220 align:middle line:84%
the value of xt given
information on times

00:38:01.220 --> 00:38:10.220 align:middle line:84%
s and before will be independent
of all of the times before.

00:38:10.220 --> 00:38:15.660 align:middle line:84%
And so the conditional
distribution,

00:38:15.660 --> 00:38:22.780 align:middle line:84%
so the distribution
of xt, given all

00:38:22.780 --> 00:38:30.420 align:middle line:84%
of the values of x prior to time
u, is equal in distribution.

00:38:30.420 --> 00:38:33.700 align:middle line:84%
It has the same
random distribution

00:38:33.700 --> 00:38:40.260 align:middle line:84%
as just the conditional
distribution given xs.

00:38:40.260 --> 00:38:47.580 align:middle line:84%
So with a Markov process, the
future depends on the past

00:38:47.580 --> 00:38:51.820 align:middle line:84%
only through the last
value of the past.

00:38:51.820 --> 00:38:56.100 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't matter
how one arrived

00:38:56.100 --> 00:39:00.360 align:middle line:90%
at a given value at time s.

00:39:00.360 --> 00:39:04.880 align:middle line:84%
For different realizations
that have the same end point,

00:39:04.880 --> 00:39:10.080 align:middle line:84%
it's only that point that
indexes the distributions

00:39:10.080 --> 00:39:11.260 align:middle line:90%
into the future.

00:39:11.260 --> 00:39:12.670 align:middle line:90%
So this is a Markov process.

00:39:12.670 --> 00:39:16.840 align:middle line:90%


00:39:16.840 --> 00:39:28.040 align:middle line:84%
It's a simple model where
the past information can

00:39:28.040 --> 00:39:32.920 align:middle line:84%
be encapsulated just in
the last observed value,

00:39:32.920 --> 00:39:38.320 align:middle line:84%
not in the realizations of
how that value was achieved.

00:39:38.320 --> 00:39:41.320 align:middle line:84%
So this is just some
notation for trying

00:39:41.320 --> 00:39:44.360 align:middle line:90%
to say what I just said.

00:39:44.360 --> 00:39:52.360 align:middle line:84%
Now, with Markov chains, these
are interesting stochastic

00:39:52.360 --> 00:40:01.460 align:middle line:84%
processes where we consider
there being a state space,

00:40:01.460 --> 00:40:08.020 align:middle line:84%
capital S, where we can index
possible states that the process

00:40:08.020 --> 00:40:12.280 align:middle line:84%
beginning, say, with
0 and the integers.

00:40:12.280 --> 00:40:17.780 align:middle line:84%
And it could be a finite number
or a countably infinite number.

00:40:17.780 --> 00:40:23.500 align:middle line:84%
And then we have a time
index set with n varying

00:40:23.500 --> 00:40:28.380 align:middle line:84%
from 0 through the
positive integers.

00:40:28.380 --> 00:40:40.620 align:middle line:84%
And so the Markov property
with this stochastic process

00:40:40.620 --> 00:40:50.300 align:middle line:84%
would have that the probability
of the next state being

00:40:50.300 --> 00:40:59.670 align:middle line:84%
j from time n, given the values
of the process x0 through xn,

00:40:59.670 --> 00:41:03.870 align:middle line:84%
this conditional
probability is equal

00:41:03.870 --> 00:41:09.390 align:middle line:84%
to the conditional
probability of j state

00:41:09.390 --> 00:41:15.590 align:middle line:84%
at time n plus 1, just
given that xn is equal to i.

00:41:15.590 --> 00:41:22.550 align:middle line:84%
So this is a nice
property for processes

00:41:22.550 --> 00:41:27.463 align:middle line:84%
that makes their analysis
a bit less complex.

00:41:27.463 --> 00:41:30.070 align:middle line:90%


00:41:30.070 --> 00:41:39.150 align:middle line:84%
And what's important in this
definition of the Markov process

00:41:39.150 --> 00:41:45.270 align:middle line:84%
is that there's a
one step transition

00:41:45.270 --> 00:41:54.390 align:middle line:84%
probability from state i
to state j, from time n

00:41:54.390 --> 00:42:02.010 align:middle line:84%
to n plus 1, and these
transition probabilities,

00:42:02.010 --> 00:42:07.050 align:middle line:84%
together with the probabilities
of the initial states,

00:42:07.050 --> 00:42:10.970 align:middle line:84%
completely specifies
the stochastic process.

00:42:10.970 --> 00:42:15.090 align:middle line:84%
Now, this issue of completely
specifying a stochastic process

00:42:15.090 --> 00:42:18.210 align:middle line:84%
is an important
one, in that what

00:42:18.210 --> 00:42:22.090 align:middle line:84%
we want with a
stochastic process

00:42:22.090 --> 00:42:24.770 align:middle line:84%
is to have a
complete probability

00:42:24.770 --> 00:42:27.330 align:middle line:90%
model of that process.

00:42:27.330 --> 00:42:35.410 align:middle line:84%
And so if we have an initial
state x0 and the probabilities

00:42:35.410 --> 00:42:42.610 align:middle line:84%
over all states i in S, that
characterizes the probability

00:42:42.610 --> 00:42:47.050 align:middle line:90%
model for the state 0 value.

00:42:47.050 --> 00:42:52.490 align:middle line:84%
Then if we look at the
conditional probability

00:42:52.490 --> 00:42:55.580 align:middle line:90%
from time n equals 0 to n--

00:42:55.580 --> 00:42:58.380 align:middle line:90%


00:42:58.380 --> 00:43:02.900 align:middle line:84%
or from 0 to 1, we then have
the conditional probabilities

00:43:02.900 --> 00:43:11.260 align:middle line:84%
of moving from any state
0 to any state at time 1.

00:43:11.260 --> 00:43:16.460 align:middle line:84%
And so those conditional
probabilities, together

00:43:16.460 --> 00:43:19.260 align:middle line:84%
with the marginal
probability, specify

00:43:19.260 --> 00:43:24.140 align:middle line:84%
the distribution of the
process over state 0 and 1.

00:43:24.140 --> 00:43:29.780 align:middle line:84%
And we can continue this
computation for successive time

00:43:29.780 --> 00:43:36.580 align:middle line:84%
points and completely specify
our stochastic process.

00:43:36.580 --> 00:43:42.100 align:middle line:84%
Now, the general Markov
process, as introduced there,

00:43:42.100 --> 00:43:46.580 align:middle line:84%
is very complex, because we
may have different transition

00:43:46.580 --> 00:43:50.780 align:middle line:84%
probabilities depending upon
how long the process has

00:43:50.780 --> 00:43:53.080 align:middle line:90%
been operating.

00:43:53.080 --> 00:43:58.940 align:middle line:84%
A simple case is called a
stationary Markov process,

00:43:58.940 --> 00:44:04.240 align:middle line:84%
where these transition
probabilities, one step

00:44:04.240 --> 00:44:09.820 align:middle line:84%
transition probabilities,
do not depend on the time.

00:44:09.820 --> 00:44:11.480 align:middle line:84%
So we have the
same probabilities

00:44:11.480 --> 00:44:13.960 align:middle line:84%
of moving from one
state to another

00:44:13.960 --> 00:44:18.440 align:middle line:84%
regardless of when
that is occurring.

00:44:18.440 --> 00:44:23.200 align:middle line:84%
And so we can define
a matrix P, which

00:44:23.200 --> 00:44:28.960 align:middle line:84%
is the collection of all
pairwise probabilities Pij

00:44:28.960 --> 00:44:34.200 align:middle line:90%
moving from state i to state j.

00:44:34.200 --> 00:44:40.600 align:middle line:84%
And we basically require that
these transition probabilities

00:44:40.600 --> 00:44:45.360 align:middle line:84%
are non-negative, and the
sum over target states

00:44:45.360 --> 00:44:49.200 align:middle line:84%
j from state i that
those probabilities

00:44:49.200 --> 00:44:56.650 align:middle line:84%
sum to 1 for a stationary
transition probability matrix.

00:44:56.650 --> 00:45:04.330 align:middle line:84%
And to define the
probability distribution

00:45:04.330 --> 00:45:10.530 align:middle line:84%
of the entire process xn using
this stationary transition

00:45:10.530 --> 00:45:14.970 align:middle line:84%
probability matrix and
initial probabilities,

00:45:14.970 --> 00:45:22.610 align:middle line:84%
we can compute any
probabilities by computing

00:45:22.610 --> 00:45:25.690 align:middle line:84%
the collection of all
such probabilities

00:45:25.690 --> 00:45:30.630 align:middle line:84%
where we have n
steps of the process,

00:45:30.630 --> 00:45:35.770 align:middle line:84%
or n time points of the
process, and states i0 through i

00:45:35.770 --> 00:45:38.030 align:middle line:90%
little n there.

00:45:38.030 --> 00:45:41.290 align:middle line:84%
So if we can calculate
these probabilities,

00:45:41.290 --> 00:45:45.250 align:middle line:84%
we will have specified
the probability model.

00:45:45.250 --> 00:45:50.950 align:middle line:84%
Now, in calculating
the joint probability

00:45:50.950 --> 00:45:59.510 align:middle line:84%
of the first little n values
states being i0 through in,

00:45:59.510 --> 00:46:02.950 align:middle line:84%
we can decompose
this probability

00:46:02.950 --> 00:46:07.190 align:middle line:84%
into the marginal
probability of the first n

00:46:07.190 --> 00:46:15.750 align:middle line:84%
minus 1 times the conditional
probability of the last given

00:46:15.750 --> 00:46:16.370 align:middle line:90%
the others.

00:46:16.370 --> 00:46:27.150 align:middle line:84%
So if we think of A
equaling x0 up to xn minus 1

00:46:27.150 --> 00:46:39.430 align:middle line:84%
equals i, n minus 1, and B
is equal to xn equal to in,

00:46:39.430 --> 00:46:46.916 align:middle line:84%
then we can look at
the probability of A

00:46:46.916 --> 00:46:52.250 align:middle line:84%
and B is equal to the
probability of A times

00:46:52.250 --> 00:46:57.250 align:middle line:90%
the probability of B given A.

00:46:57.250 --> 00:47:02.010 align:middle line:84%
And this is basically
what's written up there.

00:47:02.010 --> 00:47:05.290 align:middle line:84%
And this formula
on the Blackboard

00:47:05.290 --> 00:47:09.570 align:middle line:84%
is simply Bayes'
formula relating

00:47:09.570 --> 00:47:11.730 align:middle line:84%
the conditional
probability of B given

00:47:11.730 --> 00:47:27.850 align:middle line:84%
A to the probabilities of A and
B. So probability of B given A.

00:47:27.850 --> 00:47:32.770 align:middle line:84%
Now, with this
second equation, we

00:47:32.770 --> 00:47:37.950 align:middle line:84%
can simplify that to
the red probability,

00:47:37.950 --> 00:47:44.370 align:middle line:84%
which is only depending
upon the n minus 1 value.

00:47:44.370 --> 00:47:51.100 align:middle line:84%
And so we have that
this probability

00:47:51.100 --> 00:47:54.620 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the marginal
probability of the first n

00:47:54.620 --> 00:48:02.326 align:middle line:84%
minus 1 times the transition
probability from state

00:48:02.326 --> 00:48:05.980 align:middle line:90%
in minus 1 to in.

00:48:05.980 --> 00:48:09.220 align:middle line:90%
And that won't depend on n.

00:48:09.220 --> 00:48:15.380 align:middle line:84%
And so we can
basically, by induction,

00:48:15.380 --> 00:48:21.540 align:middle line:84%
basically replace each of the
successive state transitions

00:48:21.540 --> 00:48:25.060 align:middle line:84%
by the probability of
moving from one state

00:48:25.060 --> 00:48:29.220 align:middle line:84%
to another and the successive
transitions of all those states.

00:48:29.220 --> 00:48:36.620 align:middle line:84%
So this joint probability can
be represented in this form,

00:48:36.620 --> 00:48:39.980 align:middle line:84%
exploiting the Markov property
to simplify the one step

00:48:39.980 --> 00:48:49.120 align:middle line:84%
transitions, and using the
stationery property to define

00:48:49.120 --> 00:48:49.800 align:middle line:90%
these Pij's.

00:48:49.800 --> 00:48:52.480 align:middle line:90%


00:48:52.480 --> 00:48:59.520 align:middle line:84%
OK, well, with stationary
transition probabilities,

00:48:59.520 --> 00:49:03.040 align:middle line:84%
the multi-step
transition probabilities

00:49:03.040 --> 00:49:08.040 align:middle line:84%
turn out to have a
really nice form.

00:49:08.040 --> 00:49:16.600 align:middle line:84%
And so we can represent the
transition probability matrix

00:49:16.600 --> 00:49:22.760 align:middle line:84%
over n steps as the n-th power
of the one step transition

00:49:22.760 --> 00:49:25.960 align:middle line:90%
probabilities matrices.

00:49:25.960 --> 00:49:30.680 align:middle line:84%
So this property
of Markov chains

00:49:30.680 --> 00:49:35.400 align:middle line:84%
with stationary transition
probabilities is very nice

00:49:35.400 --> 00:49:42.250 align:middle line:90%
and gives us this result.

00:49:42.250 --> 00:49:45.610 align:middle line:90%
Now, let's see.

00:49:45.610 --> 00:49:48.130 align:middle line:84%
In terms of proving
this, There's

00:49:48.130 --> 00:49:52.850 align:middle line:84%
this analysis called
first step analysis

00:49:52.850 --> 00:49:54.930 align:middle line:90%
in stochastic processes.

00:49:54.930 --> 00:50:00.370 align:middle line:84%
That's a really useful tool
for proving different results.

00:50:00.370 --> 00:50:12.130 align:middle line:84%
And this first step
analysis basically

00:50:12.130 --> 00:50:14.770 align:middle line:84%
says, OK, if we
want to calculate

00:50:14.770 --> 00:50:18.730 align:middle line:84%
the probability of
going from state 0,

00:50:18.730 --> 00:50:24.490 align:middle line:84%
sorry, state i to j
in n steps, that's

00:50:24.490 --> 00:50:31.130 align:middle line:84%
like starting at time 0 in state
i and transitioning to state j.

00:50:31.130 --> 00:50:38.370 align:middle line:84%
Then we can decompose
this probability

00:50:38.370 --> 00:50:44.070 align:middle line:84%
into the sum over all
possible first steps going

00:50:44.070 --> 00:50:55.910 align:middle line:84%
from i to k in state 1 and then
from state k to j by time n.

00:50:55.910 --> 00:51:03.590 align:middle line:84%
And so this first equation
here is a first step equation

00:51:03.590 --> 00:51:04.890 align:middle line:90%
of the probabilities.

00:51:04.890 --> 00:51:09.750 align:middle line:84%
We can basically decompose the
process of going from state i

00:51:09.750 --> 00:51:13.430 align:middle line:84%
to j in n steps as
going from state i

00:51:13.430 --> 00:51:21.470 align:middle line:84%
to each k state in step one,
and then going from state k

00:51:21.470 --> 00:51:25.510 align:middle line:90%
to n in n minus 1 steps.

00:51:25.510 --> 00:51:33.470 align:middle line:90%
So this is that formula.

00:51:33.470 --> 00:51:47.130 align:middle line:84%
And so if we write this out, we
end up getting the xn equaling--

00:51:47.130 --> 00:51:52.610 align:middle line:84%
or this in blue is equal
to the term above in black

00:51:52.610 --> 00:51:57.770 align:middle line:84%
because of the Markov
nature of the Markov

00:51:57.770 --> 00:52:00.290 align:middle line:90%
property of the process.

00:52:00.290 --> 00:52:04.170 align:middle line:84%
And so we basically
have this sum

00:52:04.170 --> 00:52:13.940 align:middle line:84%
of Pik times probability of xn
minus 1 equaling jx0 equals k.

00:52:13.940 --> 00:52:20.090 align:middle line:90%


00:52:20.090 --> 00:52:25.010 align:middle line:84%
So we end up basically getting
this matrix equation of the n

00:52:25.010 --> 00:52:29.850 align:middle line:84%
step transition probabilities
is equal to the one step matrix

00:52:29.850 --> 00:52:33.570 align:middle line:84%
P times the n minus 1 step
transition probability

00:52:33.570 --> 00:52:36.170 align:middle line:90%
matrix in green.

00:52:36.170 --> 00:52:36.930 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:52:36.930 --> 00:52:41.440 align:middle line:84%
Well, these transition
probabilities,

00:52:41.440 --> 00:52:44.580 align:middle line:84%
together with the
marginal distribution

00:52:44.580 --> 00:52:47.940 align:middle line:84%
of the initial state, gives
us the marginal distribution

00:52:47.940 --> 00:52:50.670 align:middle line:90%
of xn at any time.

00:52:50.670 --> 00:52:53.640 align:middle line:90%


00:52:53.640 --> 00:52:57.820 align:middle line:84%
So these formulas just indicate
how we do these computations.

00:52:57.820 --> 00:53:02.900 align:middle line:84%
Now, where do Markov
chains arise in finance?

00:53:02.900 --> 00:53:07.100 align:middle line:84%
Well, one example to
think about is how

00:53:07.100 --> 00:53:11.060 align:middle line:90%
companies have credit ratings.

00:53:11.060 --> 00:53:17.940 align:middle line:84%
And those credit ratings range
from high credit, which is AAA,

00:53:17.940 --> 00:53:23.780 align:middle line:84%
and low credit being I
guess C. D being default

00:53:23.780 --> 00:53:29.820 align:middle line:84%
is maybe a terminal state,
at least in some sense.

00:53:29.820 --> 00:53:34.580 align:middle line:84%
But companies are
graded or rated

00:53:34.580 --> 00:53:40.560 align:middle line:84%
by Standard and Poor's and
Fitch and other rating agencies,

00:53:40.560 --> 00:53:44.440 align:middle line:84%
and depending upon how good
their credit rating is,

00:53:44.440 --> 00:53:50.600 align:middle line:84%
they can borrow money
at lower interest rates.

00:53:50.600 --> 00:53:54.000 align:middle line:84%
And the way corporations
borrow money

00:53:54.000 --> 00:53:57.600 align:middle line:84%
is to issue a bond, paying
a certain interest rate

00:53:57.600 --> 00:54:04.560 align:middle line:84%
and having that interest rate
be attractive to lenders.

00:54:04.560 --> 00:54:10.440 align:middle line:84%
And the way that
such a loan contract

00:54:10.440 --> 00:54:15.280 align:middle line:84%
is attractive to lenders
is with a high interest

00:54:15.280 --> 00:54:24.680 align:middle line:84%
rate to the lender, but a low
risk of default by the borrower.

00:54:24.680 --> 00:54:32.360 align:middle line:84%
And so one can think about
how these credit ratings vary

00:54:32.360 --> 00:54:36.210 align:middle line:90%
from one period to the next.

00:54:36.210 --> 00:54:42.970 align:middle line:84%
And here's a table of
migration probabilities

00:54:42.970 --> 00:54:45.930 align:middle line:90%
from one year to the next.

00:54:45.930 --> 00:54:52.170 align:middle line:84%
And one can see that
AAA initial ratings

00:54:52.170 --> 00:54:59.650 align:middle line:84%
had only a 43% chance of staying
AAA at the end of that one year.

00:54:59.650 --> 00:55:02.410 align:middle line:84%
And there was a
very low probability

00:55:02.410 --> 00:55:09.130 align:middle line:90%
of downgrades to C, CCC, or B.

00:55:09.130 --> 00:55:16.810 align:middle line:84%
And with, say, very low credit
rating companies, the chance

00:55:16.810 --> 00:55:22.610 align:middle line:84%
of basically increasing
their credit rating to B

00:55:22.610 --> 00:55:28.650 align:middle line:84%
was 41%, staying at
CCC is 32%, but almost

00:55:28.650 --> 00:55:31.890 align:middle line:84%
a 20% chance of
defaulting perhaps.

00:55:31.890 --> 00:55:39.030 align:middle line:84%
So these kinds of
metrics basically

00:55:39.030 --> 00:55:48.190 align:middle line:84%
can be analyzed with the
tools of Markov chains.

00:55:48.190 --> 00:55:50.270 align:middle line:90%
And let's see.

00:55:50.270 --> 00:55:54.510 align:middle line:84%
Then another example
that I put together

00:55:54.510 --> 00:56:02.170 align:middle line:84%
is looking at the stock
price dynamics of any stock,

00:56:02.170 --> 00:56:07.150 align:middle line:84%
but I chose Apple,
and using daily prices

00:56:07.150 --> 00:56:14.390 align:middle line:84%
to consider states of the
Apple stock price, which

00:56:14.390 --> 00:56:18.110 align:middle line:90%
is it an up day or a down day?

00:56:18.110 --> 00:56:22.470 align:middle line:84%
Is the price at day t
higher than t minus 1,

00:56:22.470 --> 00:56:24.810 align:middle line:90%
or is it a down day?

00:56:24.810 --> 00:56:26.310 align:middle line:84%
I guess one of those
should probably

00:56:26.310 --> 00:56:30.910 align:middle line:84%
be a less than or equal to
if we wanted to incorporate

00:56:30.910 --> 00:56:32.730 align:middle line:90%
corporate everything.

00:56:32.730 --> 00:56:42.650 align:middle line:84%
But we can consider defining
a two day state, which,

00:56:42.650 --> 00:56:44.910 align:middle line:84%
let's see, the formatting
here is a little off,

00:56:44.910 --> 00:56:49.250 align:middle line:84%
but we could have
two up days in a row,

00:56:49.250 --> 00:56:52.610 align:middle line:84%
an up day followed
by a down day,

00:56:52.610 --> 00:56:54.730 align:middle line:84%
a down day followed
by an up day,

00:56:54.730 --> 00:56:57.770 align:middle line:84%
and a down day
followed by a down day.

00:56:57.770 --> 00:57:04.930 align:middle line:84%
And look at trying to model
the state changes for a two day

00:57:04.930 --> 00:57:09.690 align:middle line:90%
state with a Markov chain.

00:57:09.690 --> 00:57:14.130 align:middle line:84%
And in R, there's
a nice r package

00:57:14.130 --> 00:57:17.890 align:middle line:90%
that will do this for us.

00:57:17.890 --> 00:57:23.490 align:middle line:84%
And this was distributed
in the Canvas site.

00:57:23.490 --> 00:57:25.650 align:middle line:84%
And so you should be
able to replicate this

00:57:25.650 --> 00:57:30.740 align:middle line:84%
example with Apple or
choose any other stock

00:57:30.740 --> 00:57:32.780 align:middle line:90%
you might be interested in.

00:57:32.780 --> 00:57:41.766 align:middle line:84%
And so importantly, this uses
the library quantmod here.

00:57:41.766 --> 00:57:44.420 align:middle line:90%
Sort of hard to see.

00:57:44.420 --> 00:57:45.540 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:57:45.540 --> 00:57:48.260 align:middle line:84%
So there's a library
quantmod, which

00:57:48.260 --> 00:57:52.060 align:middle line:84%
allows us to collect price
data from the internet.

00:57:52.060 --> 00:57:57.120 align:middle line:84%
And then there's this
package Markov chain,

00:57:57.120 --> 00:58:03.060 align:middle line:84%
which allows us to specify
Markov chain models.

00:58:03.060 --> 00:58:09.780 align:middle line:84%
And so with this, our
code, it basically

00:58:09.780 --> 00:58:16.380 align:middle line:84%
collects the data
on Apple stock.

00:58:16.380 --> 00:58:21.340 align:middle line:84%
And I think what I really
want to emphasize for everyone

00:58:21.340 --> 00:58:23.360 align:middle line:90%
is that these examples--

00:58:23.360 --> 00:58:25.660 align:middle line:84%
and so it's a bit tedious
if you want to go through

00:58:25.660 --> 00:58:27.080 align:middle line:90%
these examples yourself.

00:58:27.080 --> 00:58:31.160 align:middle line:84%
However, the examples
should work just

00:58:31.160 --> 00:58:33.120 align:middle line:84%
with clicking a
button, saying run,

00:58:33.120 --> 00:58:35.640 align:middle line:90%
and you can replicate these.

00:58:35.640 --> 00:58:38.080 align:middle line:84%
And so for the
Apple stock, we just

00:58:38.080 --> 00:58:42.920 align:middle line:84%
needed to substitute
in the Apple symbol

00:58:42.920 --> 00:58:45.160 align:middle line:90%
and collect the data.

00:58:45.160 --> 00:58:51.640 align:middle line:84%
And so here's the Apple
stock price from 2014 or 2013

00:58:51.640 --> 00:58:56.040 align:middle line:90%
through a week ago.

00:58:56.040 --> 00:59:04.440 align:middle line:84%
And I considered just fitting a
Markov chain model for these up

00:59:04.440 --> 00:59:09.320 align:middle line:84%
and down states to the
last 4 and 1/2 years.

00:59:09.320 --> 00:59:13.920 align:middle line:84%
And so the code
here goes through.

00:59:13.920 --> 00:59:19.840 align:middle line:84%
And basically,
with Apple prices,

00:59:19.840 --> 00:59:24.200 align:middle line:84%
Apple closing prices here,
we define differences

00:59:24.200 --> 00:59:27.170 align:middle line:90%
in closing prices for two lags.

00:59:27.170 --> 00:59:36.050 align:middle line:84%
And then talk about defining
up, up days in this section

00:59:36.050 --> 00:59:41.610 align:middle line:84%
here, up down days,
down up, and so forth.

00:59:41.610 --> 00:59:46.790 align:middle line:84%
And then if we graph
these states, say,

00:59:46.790 --> 00:59:50.490 align:middle line:84%
from May of this year
to the end of August,

00:59:50.490 --> 00:59:56.250 align:middle line:84%
this shows how
this 4 state Markov

00:59:56.250 --> 01:00:01.490 align:middle line:90%
chain evolved over these dates.

01:00:01.490 --> 01:00:10.816 align:middle line:84%
And we can just use the
Markov chain function.

01:00:10.816 --> 01:00:16.010 align:middle line:90%


01:00:16.010 --> 01:00:16.950 align:middle line:90%
Or let's see.

01:00:16.950 --> 01:00:23.970 align:middle line:84%
We can plot a Markov chain
function or a Markov chain

01:00:23.970 --> 01:00:29.170 align:middle line:84%
transition states
as this graph here.

01:00:29.170 --> 01:00:32.630 align:middle line:84%
So what this code does is
it looks at transitions

01:00:32.630 --> 01:00:35.590 align:middle line:84%
from one day to the
next and calculates

01:00:35.590 --> 01:00:39.150 align:middle line:84%
the probabilities of going
from one state to another.

01:00:39.150 --> 01:00:43.150 align:middle line:84%
So the sums across
rows equals 1.

01:00:43.150 --> 01:00:49.070 align:middle line:84%
And this graph, this
plot shows basically

01:00:49.070 --> 01:00:51.290 align:middle line:84%
what is happening
with the Markov chain.

01:00:51.290 --> 01:00:56.110 align:middle line:84%
We have different states
given by the nodes.

01:00:56.110 --> 01:01:00.070 align:middle line:84%
And then we can transition
from between nodes

01:01:00.070 --> 01:01:02.990 align:middle line:90%
by which arrows are feasible.

01:01:02.990 --> 01:01:07.950 align:middle line:84%
And this Markov chain
package will give us

01:01:07.950 --> 01:01:10.750 align:middle line:84%
the probabilities,
empirical probabilities,

01:01:10.750 --> 01:01:13.550 align:middle line:84%
of moving from one
state to another.

01:01:13.550 --> 01:01:20.870 align:middle line:84%
And so one can see that
two up days in Apple

01:01:20.870 --> 01:01:27.350 align:middle line:84%
are likely to be followed
by two up days, again,

01:01:27.350 --> 01:01:32.290 align:middle line:84%
so that we'd get a third up day
with that one step transition.

01:01:32.290 --> 01:01:37.830 align:middle line:84%
Two down days are not likely to
be followed by a third down day,

01:01:37.830 --> 01:01:43.290 align:middle line:84%
because it won't go to
that down, down state

01:01:43.290 --> 01:01:49.410 align:middle line:84%
with probability
or more than 0.5.

01:01:49.410 --> 01:01:53.210 align:middle line:84%
So there tends to perhaps be
reversals from double down days

01:01:53.210 --> 01:02:01.090 align:middle line:84%
and a continuation of up days,
as suggested by these graphs.

01:02:01.090 --> 01:02:08.250 align:middle line:84%
Now, with this example, I
thought, well, maybe there

01:02:08.250 --> 01:02:09.850 align:middle line:84%
would be some
interesting structure

01:02:09.850 --> 01:02:14.370 align:middle line:90%
if we defined three day states.

01:02:14.370 --> 01:02:23.060 align:middle line:84%
And so the same logic gives
us 8 states of up or down

01:02:23.060 --> 01:02:26.060 align:middle line:90%
on 3 successive days.

01:02:26.060 --> 01:02:31.380 align:middle line:84%
And so 2 times 2 times 2,
or 8, is the total number

01:02:31.380 --> 01:02:33.660 align:middle line:90%
of possible states.

01:02:33.660 --> 01:02:39.100 align:middle line:84%
And one can calculate the
empirical probabilities

01:02:39.100 --> 01:02:50.020 align:middle line:84%
of transitioning and then get
this display of the Markov chain

01:02:50.020 --> 01:02:52.660 align:middle line:90%
with these eight states.

01:02:52.660 --> 01:02:57.700 align:middle line:84%
And so what's
rather curious just

01:02:57.700 --> 01:03:00.660 align:middle line:84%
to focus on our three
down days are not

01:03:00.660 --> 01:03:03.620 align:middle line:84%
likely to be
followed by a fourth.

01:03:03.620 --> 01:03:06.340 align:middle line:84%
And three up days
maybe are likely to be

01:03:06.340 --> 01:03:09.340 align:middle line:90%
followed by a fourth up day.

01:03:09.340 --> 01:03:12.660 align:middle line:84%
So you've got some
insight perhaps

01:03:12.660 --> 01:03:15.360 align:middle line:90%
into the overall dynamics.

01:03:15.360 --> 01:03:23.320 align:middle line:84%
And what's great to me is that
we can visualize the process

01:03:23.320 --> 01:03:30.160 align:middle line:84%
and the graphical representation
of nodes and edges

01:03:30.160 --> 01:03:35.070 align:middle line:84%
between the nodes that have
probabilities is quite useful.

01:03:35.070 --> 01:03:37.600 align:middle line:90%


01:03:37.600 --> 01:03:39.000 align:middle line:90%
So let's see.

01:03:39.000 --> 01:03:46.440 align:middle line:84%
If you study Markov
chains and then

01:03:46.440 --> 01:03:52.400 align:middle line:84%
one can classify different
states or different nodes

01:03:52.400 --> 01:03:55.280 align:middle line:90%
as communicating states.

01:03:55.280 --> 01:03:59.280 align:middle line:84%
So can you move from one
state to another or not?

01:03:59.280 --> 01:04:05.100 align:middle line:84%
And in this case, one can move
from one state to another,

01:04:05.100 --> 01:04:09.500 align:middle line:84%
possibly not in one time
period, but in multiple ones.

01:04:09.500 --> 01:04:13.280 align:middle line:84%
And so this happens to be
a strongly connected set

01:04:13.280 --> 01:04:14.760 align:middle line:90%
of states.

01:04:14.760 --> 01:04:17.100 align:middle line:90%
And let's see.

01:04:17.100 --> 01:04:20.430 align:middle line:90%


01:04:20.430 --> 01:04:24.730 align:middle line:84%
This little note ends
with printing out

01:04:24.730 --> 01:04:32.290 align:middle line:84%
the columns of the
transition probability matrix

01:04:32.290 --> 01:04:38.810 align:middle line:90%
for up moves and down moves.

01:04:38.810 --> 01:04:46.090 align:middle line:84%
And so if we wanted
to move from one state

01:04:46.090 --> 01:04:52.290 align:middle line:84%
to an up move in
the next period,

01:04:52.290 --> 01:04:55.210 align:middle line:84%
then looking at
this table, one can

01:04:55.210 --> 01:05:04.930 align:middle line:84%
see that the highest probability
of the three day state being up

01:05:04.930 --> 01:05:15.850 align:middle line:84%
in the third day, given the
prior three day state, then

01:05:15.850 --> 01:05:18.630 align:middle line:84%
it corresponds to
three down days

01:05:18.630 --> 01:05:24.830 align:middle line:84%
lead to an up day the next
day with almost a 60% chance.

01:05:24.830 --> 01:05:30.470 align:middle line:84%
And one can also do
the same for down moves

01:05:30.470 --> 01:05:35.950 align:middle line:84%
and see under what state are we
most likely to have a down move.

01:05:35.950 --> 01:05:43.350 align:middle line:84%
And so I think 0.510
is the highest,

01:05:43.350 --> 01:05:46.230 align:middle line:84%
which isn't much
higher than 0.5.

01:05:46.230 --> 01:05:52.750 align:middle line:84%
But basically, the past
few days being a bit mixed,

01:05:52.750 --> 01:05:57.270 align:middle line:84%
but with two up days
preceded by a down day,

01:05:57.270 --> 01:06:02.910 align:middle line:84%
give us a higher
likelihood of a down day.

01:06:02.910 --> 01:06:06.710 align:middle line:90%
So let's see.

01:06:06.710 --> 01:06:13.150 align:middle line:84%
With this note, let's
see, there's a--

01:06:13.150 --> 01:06:18.810 align:middle line:84%
OK, so here's the reference
for this Markov chain package.

01:06:18.810 --> 01:06:22.050 align:middle line:84%
And what's quite
convenient with different R

01:06:22.050 --> 01:06:26.570 align:middle line:84%
packages on different
application topics

01:06:26.570 --> 01:06:32.490 align:middle line:84%
is that these articles often
go through the mathematical

01:06:32.490 --> 01:06:34.110 align:middle line:90%
background of the models.

01:06:34.110 --> 01:06:39.410 align:middle line:84%
And so if you check out this
discussion, this article

01:06:39.410 --> 01:06:42.570 align:middle line:84%
on the Markov chain
package, it actually

01:06:42.570 --> 01:06:46.330 align:middle line:84%
will include the
mathematical issues

01:06:46.330 --> 01:06:49.930 align:middle line:84%
of how you might
classify different states

01:06:49.930 --> 01:06:52.030 align:middle line:90%
and do various computations.

01:06:52.030 --> 01:06:57.120 align:middle line:84%
So that's quite useful to
have as a resource with these.

01:06:57.120 --> 01:06:59.650 align:middle line:90%


01:06:59.650 --> 01:07:01.450 align:middle line:90%
Now, let's see.

01:07:01.450 --> 01:07:09.030 align:middle line:84%
With the RStudio Cloud
files that were distributed,

01:07:09.030 --> 01:07:15.220 align:middle line:84%
if you use those programs,
you can change the symbols

01:07:15.220 --> 01:07:19.060 align:middle line:84%
from Apple to any other and
use different time periods

01:07:19.060 --> 01:07:22.460 align:middle line:84%
to see whether these
properties may be strong or not

01:07:22.460 --> 01:07:23.360 align:middle line:90%
with other stocks.

01:07:23.360 --> 01:07:27.500 align:middle line:84%
And I think that might be an
interesting thing to check out.

01:07:27.500 --> 01:07:33.940 align:middle line:84%
Our next topic, which is
on regression analysis.

01:07:33.940 --> 01:07:41.380 align:middle line:84%
And with regression,
we want to just review

01:07:41.380 --> 01:07:47.580 align:middle line:84%
the notation and setup for
multiple linear regression.

01:07:47.580 --> 01:07:52.020 align:middle line:84%
If you've taken any
statistics class

01:07:52.020 --> 01:07:55.100 align:middle line:84%
prior to this, which
most of you have,

01:07:55.100 --> 01:07:57.820 align:middle line:84%
you're very familiar with
simple linear regression where

01:07:57.820 --> 01:08:01.860 align:middle line:84%
you have one dependent
variable and one

01:08:01.860 --> 01:08:05.380 align:middle line:84%
predictor or
explanatory variable.

01:08:05.380 --> 01:08:09.660 align:middle line:84%
We want to consider the case
of multiple linear regression,

01:08:09.660 --> 01:08:17.160 align:middle line:84%
where we have a single dependent
variable and multiple predictor

01:08:17.160 --> 01:08:21.479 align:middle line:84%
or explanatory variables
given by x's, and we

01:08:21.479 --> 01:08:24.000 align:middle line:84%
want to describe
the relationship

01:08:24.000 --> 01:08:28.220 align:middle line:84%
between the dependent and
independent variables.

01:08:28.220 --> 01:08:31.640 align:middle line:84%
Now, different
regression problems

01:08:31.640 --> 01:08:34.160 align:middle line:90%
can focus on prediction.

01:08:34.160 --> 01:08:38.319 align:middle line:84%
This is actually one that I
find most engaging, at least

01:08:38.319 --> 01:08:44.080 align:middle line:84%
trying to, say, predict stock
prices over fixed horizons

01:08:44.080 --> 01:08:46.640 align:middle line:90%
is of real interest.

01:08:46.640 --> 01:08:49.200 align:middle line:84%
But regression
models can also be

01:08:49.200 --> 01:08:55.200 align:middle line:84%
used to make inferences
of causality.

01:08:55.200 --> 01:09:02.279 align:middle line:84%
Regression models arise
in medical experiments

01:09:02.279 --> 01:09:04.960 align:middle line:84%
where one of the
explanatory variables

01:09:04.960 --> 01:09:07.359 align:middle line:84%
corresponds to a
treatment effect.

01:09:07.359 --> 01:09:09.840 align:middle line:84%
One can also be
interested in trying

01:09:09.840 --> 01:09:15.410 align:middle line:84%
to approximate the dependent
variable by functions

01:09:15.410 --> 01:09:18.569 align:middle line:84%
of the independent
variables and understand

01:09:18.569 --> 01:09:21.490 align:middle line:90%
their functional relationships.

01:09:21.490 --> 01:09:30.170 align:middle line:84%
So all of these objectives can
be achieved with regression.

01:09:30.170 --> 01:09:41.250 align:middle line:84%
And we define a general linear
model with these equations here.

01:09:41.250 --> 01:09:45.770 align:middle line:84%
We basically have our
observed yi values

01:09:45.770 --> 01:09:51.410 align:middle line:84%
being a sum of a fitted
value plus error.

01:09:51.410 --> 01:09:58.210 align:middle line:84%
And the fitted value y
hat i is written here

01:09:58.210 --> 01:10:03.250 align:middle line:84%
as a linear function of
the independent variables

01:10:03.250 --> 01:10:09.990 align:middle line:84%
xi with coefficients
beta 1 through beta p.

01:10:09.990 --> 01:10:12.770 align:middle line:84%
And there's actually
a typo there.

01:10:12.770 --> 01:10:16.510 align:middle line:84%
It shouldn't be beta sub ip,
it should just be beta p there.

01:10:16.510 --> 01:10:21.910 align:middle line:84%
And with this setup,
well, we could

01:10:21.910 --> 01:10:26.470 align:middle line:84%
consider different
explanatory variables

01:10:26.470 --> 01:10:32.630 align:middle line:84%
that happen to be just
different powers of one

01:10:32.630 --> 01:10:35.270 align:middle line:90%
independent variable x.

01:10:35.270 --> 01:10:45.390 align:middle line:84%
We could also consider
Fourier series of x variables.

01:10:45.390 --> 01:10:47.990 align:middle line:84%
We could also have
time series regressions

01:10:47.990 --> 01:10:55.990 align:middle line:84%
where the independent variables
x may correspond to prior time

01:10:55.990 --> 01:10:59.230 align:middle line:90%
values of the y series.

01:10:59.230 --> 01:11:03.590 align:middle line:84%
And in all of these cases,
what's really important

01:11:03.590 --> 01:11:09.130 align:middle line:84%
is linearity of the
fitted value y hat.

01:11:09.130 --> 01:11:18.450 align:middle line:84%
And what's linear
is not that it's

01:11:18.450 --> 01:11:23.790 align:middle line:84%
the sum of x's so much as it
is the regression parameters

01:11:23.790 --> 01:11:28.290 align:middle line:84%
beta are a linear
function of those.

01:11:28.290 --> 01:11:33.610 align:middle line:84%
So the linearity of y hat
in the regression parameters

01:11:33.610 --> 01:11:41.810 align:middle line:84%
is a key property of this model,
and that linearity dependence

01:11:41.810 --> 01:11:46.210 align:middle line:84%
on the parameters allows us
to specify the parameters.

01:11:46.210 --> 01:11:55.130 align:middle line:84%
Now, let me just go to
another example here.

01:11:55.130 --> 01:11:55.900 align:middle line:90%
Let's see here.

01:11:55.900 --> 01:11:59.370 align:middle line:90%


01:11:59.370 --> 01:12:00.290 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:12:00.290 --> 01:12:03.730 align:middle line:84%
Here's a time series
of the high yield

01:12:03.730 --> 01:12:08.580 align:middle line:90%
spread in the bond markets.

01:12:08.580 --> 01:12:12.620 align:middle line:84%
And so this is looking
at the yield on bonds

01:12:12.620 --> 01:12:18.340 align:middle line:84%
with a credit rating of B
AA minus the yield on a US

01:12:18.340 --> 01:12:19.900 align:middle line:90%
Treasury.

01:12:19.900 --> 01:12:26.100 align:middle line:84%
And so this difference
in yield tends

01:12:26.100 --> 01:12:28.380 align:middle line:90%
to be positive, well above 2.

01:12:28.380 --> 01:12:32.060 align:middle line:84%
It means that these
lower credit rated

01:12:32.060 --> 01:12:37.460 align:middle line:84%
companies have to pay more
interest to lenders when

01:12:37.460 --> 01:12:39.180 align:middle line:90%
they borrow money.

01:12:39.180 --> 01:12:44.220 align:middle line:84%
But that excess rate
above the Treasury

01:12:44.220 --> 01:12:52.380 align:middle line:84%
evolves over time and
sometimes drops moderately

01:12:52.380 --> 01:12:54.380 align:middle line:90%
or rises moderately.

01:12:54.380 --> 01:12:58.100 align:middle line:84%
Now, if we were to
use just polynomials

01:12:58.100 --> 01:13:04.640 align:middle line:84%
to try and fit these, well,
as we extend to eighth order

01:13:04.640 --> 01:13:07.520 align:middle line:84%
polynomials, everyone
here probably knows

01:13:07.520 --> 01:13:09.460 align:middle line:84%
that with a high enough
order polynomial,

01:13:09.460 --> 01:13:12.360 align:middle line:90%
you can match any smooth curve.

01:13:12.360 --> 01:13:14.280 align:middle line:90%
No surprise.

01:13:14.280 --> 01:13:18.360 align:middle line:84%
Do we expect this spread to
actually follow some high order

01:13:18.360 --> 01:13:19.720 align:middle line:90%
polynomial?

01:13:19.720 --> 01:13:20.670 align:middle line:90%
Probably not.

01:13:20.670 --> 01:13:24.720 align:middle line:90%


01:13:24.720 --> 01:13:29.280 align:middle line:84%
Here's another example
of using Fourier series

01:13:29.280 --> 01:13:34.360 align:middle line:84%
with the same
spread time series.

01:13:34.360 --> 01:13:38.800 align:middle line:84%
And so as we add in more
terms in the Fourier series,

01:13:38.800 --> 01:13:44.000 align:middle line:84%
we're able to model higher
frequency variations.

01:13:44.000 --> 01:13:51.120 align:middle line:84%
And what this shows
is just how, I guess,

01:13:51.120 --> 01:13:54.680 align:middle line:84%
frequency analysis
in financial series

01:13:54.680 --> 01:13:56.110 align:middle line:90%
potentially can be useful.

01:13:56.110 --> 01:13:58.720 align:middle line:90%


01:13:58.720 --> 01:14:02.420 align:middle line:84%
What's really
surprising, though,

01:14:02.420 --> 01:14:10.490 align:middle line:84%
is if we do a autoregression,
where here is the high yield

01:14:10.490 --> 01:14:18.010 align:middle line:84%
spread, and then we basically
have an autoregression

01:14:18.010 --> 01:14:23.690 align:middle line:84%
saying that the spread tomorrow
is the same as the spread today

01:14:23.690 --> 01:14:25.330 align:middle line:90%
or very, very close.

01:14:25.330 --> 01:14:29.810 align:middle line:84%
Then we get this
blue curve fitting

01:14:29.810 --> 01:14:33.550 align:middle line:84%
the black curve, which is over,
underneath the blue curve.

01:14:33.550 --> 01:14:40.570 align:middle line:84%
So we get almost a perfect
fit with this autoregression.

01:14:40.570 --> 01:14:47.450 align:middle line:84%
So there's a multiple r squared,
which we will introduce you to,

01:14:47.450 --> 01:14:51.990 align:middle line:90%
of 0.995 for the autoregression.

01:14:51.990 --> 01:14:58.330 align:middle line:84%
So the use of historical
values of a time series

01:14:58.330 --> 01:15:02.510 align:middle line:84%
can be very powerful in
predicting future values.

01:15:02.510 --> 01:15:03.130 align:middle line:90%
All right.

01:15:03.130 --> 01:15:06.010 align:middle line:84%
So in fitting a multiple
regression model,

01:15:06.010 --> 01:15:11.310 align:middle line:84%
whether it's with time series
factors or not, what we do

01:15:11.310 --> 01:15:17.070 align:middle line:84%
is go through these steps
of proposing a model,

01:15:17.070 --> 01:15:22.870 align:middle line:84%
step one, and make assumptions
about the error term epsilon.

01:15:22.870 --> 01:15:26.550 align:middle line:84%
And then second, we
define a criterion

01:15:26.550 --> 01:15:30.110 align:middle line:84%
for judging
different estimators.

01:15:30.110 --> 01:15:34.550 align:middle line:84%
And that criterion can be
things like least squares

01:15:34.550 --> 01:15:40.310 align:middle line:84%
or minimum mean squared
prediction error or others.

01:15:40.310 --> 01:15:46.230 align:middle line:84%
And then characterize the best
estimator using that criterion.

01:15:46.230 --> 01:15:50.990 align:middle line:84%
Then we check whether
the assumptions we made

01:15:50.990 --> 01:15:54.230 align:middle line:90%
are, in fact, satisfied.

01:15:54.230 --> 01:15:59.910 align:middle line:84%
And very often, we'll discover
that certain assumptions we make

01:15:59.910 --> 01:16:05.090 align:middle line:84%
are not satisfied and we need
to revise those and create

01:16:05.090 --> 01:16:05.740 align:middle line:90%
a new model.

01:16:05.740 --> 01:16:12.210 align:middle line:90%


01:16:12.210 --> 01:16:18.530 align:middle line:84%
The model assumptions
we apply include

01:16:18.530 --> 01:16:21.410 align:middle line:84%
what are called Gauss-Markov
assumptions, which

01:16:21.410 --> 01:16:26.610 align:middle line:84%
is where we assume that
the errors in the model

01:16:26.610 --> 01:16:32.150 align:middle line:84%
have mean 0 and
constant variance.

01:16:32.150 --> 01:16:38.610 align:middle line:90%


01:16:38.610 --> 01:16:44.690 align:middle line:84%
And so with this epsilon
hat corresponding

01:16:44.690 --> 01:16:51.730 align:middle line:84%
to the n cases in the
series, we basically

01:16:51.730 --> 01:16:57.490 align:middle line:84%
can assume these are mean 0 and
have standard deviation sigma

01:16:57.490 --> 01:17:01.880 align:middle line:84%
that's a constant and that
these are uncorrelated.

01:17:01.880 --> 01:17:07.700 align:middle line:90%


01:17:07.700 --> 01:17:11.700 align:middle line:84%
We could additionally assume
that these errors are normally

01:17:11.700 --> 01:17:15.620 align:middle line:90%
distributed or not.

01:17:15.620 --> 01:17:18.060 align:middle line:84%
We can generalize
the Gauss-Markov

01:17:18.060 --> 01:17:22.660 align:middle line:84%
to assume non-constant
variances of the errors

01:17:22.660 --> 01:17:26.140 align:middle line:84%
and possibly correlations
of errors over time.

01:17:26.140 --> 01:17:28.740 align:middle line:84%
We could also consider
non-normal non-Gaussian

01:17:28.740 --> 01:17:30.180 align:middle line:90%
distributions.

01:17:30.180 --> 01:17:32.380 align:middle line:84%
And all of these
cases actually have

01:17:32.380 --> 01:17:37.060 align:middle line:90%
a role in different contexts.

01:17:37.060 --> 01:17:40.580 align:middle line:84%
In particular, I guess
generalized Gauss-Markov methods

01:17:40.580 --> 01:17:43.700 align:middle line:84%
apply with time
series regressions,

01:17:43.700 --> 01:17:49.860 align:middle line:84%
and the non-normal non-Gaussian
distributions arise as well,

01:17:49.860 --> 01:17:53.260 align:middle line:90%
often with asset returns data.

01:17:53.260 --> 01:17:56.780 align:middle line:84%
Now, in terms of what
kind of criterion

01:17:56.780 --> 01:17:59.560 align:middle line:84%
we should use for
specifying parameters,

01:17:59.560 --> 01:18:03.900 align:middle line:84%
we have least squares, maximum
likelihood, robust methods,

01:18:03.900 --> 01:18:06.860 align:middle line:84%
Bayes' methods, a whole
variety of methods.

01:18:06.860 --> 01:18:09.040 align:middle line:84%
And what's really
important to me

01:18:09.040 --> 01:18:14.600 align:middle line:84%
is for people to have
an understanding of what

01:18:14.600 --> 01:18:17.960 align:middle line:84%
these different methods
are, and when you would

01:18:17.960 --> 01:18:20.920 align:middle line:90%
prefer one method over another.

01:18:20.920 --> 01:18:24.320 align:middle line:84%
And so what I think is a
really important question

01:18:24.320 --> 01:18:29.520 align:middle line:84%
to ask when doing empirical
analyses with different models

01:18:29.520 --> 01:18:36.200 align:middle line:84%
is, are your assumptions
reasonable, if not

01:18:36.200 --> 01:18:38.760 align:middle line:84%
satisfied by the
data you're modeling?

01:18:38.760 --> 01:18:44.040 align:middle line:84%
And with different criteria for
judging different specifications

01:18:44.040 --> 01:18:50.440 align:middle line:84%
of models, are you using an
appropriate criterion or not?

01:18:50.440 --> 01:18:53.120 align:middle line:84%
Are there variations
in that criterion

01:18:53.120 --> 01:18:57.850 align:middle line:84%
that would lead to higher
performance levels?

01:18:57.850 --> 01:19:01.210 align:middle line:84%
So that for me is
a guiding question

01:19:01.210 --> 01:19:03.330 align:middle line:84%
throughout these
different checks.

01:19:03.330 --> 01:19:07.110 align:middle line:84%
So with that,
we'll finish today.

01:19:07.110 --> 01:19:11.090 align:middle line:84%
And next time, we'll
finish these notes

01:19:11.090 --> 01:19:16.130 align:middle line:84%
going through ordinary
least squares estimation.

01:19:16.130 --> 01:19:21.610 align:middle line:84%
And what's useful about this
development of least squares

01:19:21.610 --> 01:19:26.130 align:middle line:84%
estimation is that it does
so with linear algebra.

01:19:26.130 --> 01:19:29.730 align:middle line:84%
And what we'll see is
that with linear algebra,

01:19:29.730 --> 01:19:33.050 align:middle line:84%
we get very straightforward
formulas for least squares

01:19:33.050 --> 01:19:37.170 align:middle line:84%
estimates, as well as
interesting analytical

01:19:37.170 --> 01:19:39.910 align:middle line:84%
properties of those
least squares estimates.

01:19:39.910 --> 01:19:43.560 align:middle line:90%
So we'll cover that next time.

01:19:43.560 --> 01:19:55.000 align:middle line:90%